<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achelous (river)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">Achelous</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Achelous is the longest river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, flowing about 220 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindus">Pindus</a> mountains to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a>. In antiquity it formed the boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> worshipped it as a river god who in myth wrestled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. Its silt has built up a wide delta at its mouth.</p></div>
